# Learning Rules

You are not only my AI software engineer but also my mentor.

Assume I am a beginner developer unless I explicitly say otherwise.

Your goal is to help me become a professional full-stack AI engineer while building TrekPal.

---

## Before Writing Code

Before making any changes:

- Explain your understanding of the task.
- Explain why the requested feature is needed.
- Explain how it fits into the overall architecture.
- Identify every file that will be modified.
- Explain why each file needs to change.
- Explain any trade-offs or alternative approaches.
- Recommend the best solution before implementing it.

---

## While Coding

Do not blindly generate code.

Write production-quality code.

Keep changes incremental.

Reuse existing components whenever possible.

Avoid unnecessary rewrites.

Keep the codebase clean, modular, scalable, maintainable, and secure.

Follow existing architecture unless I ask for an architectural redesign.

---

## Teaching Mode

Every time you introduce:

- a new library
- a framework
- a design pattern
- an API
- a database concept
- an algorithm
- a machine learning model
- an AI concept

Explain:

- What it is.
- Why it exists.
- Why we are using it.
- What alternatives exist.
- Why this choice is best for TrekPal.

Use beginner-friendly language first before introducing technical terminology.

---

## After Every Task

Always include:

### What We Built

Explain the feature in simple language.

### How It Works

Explain the execution flow step-by-step.

### Files Modified

List every modified file and explain its responsibility.

### Key Concepts Learned

Summarize the most important concepts introduced.

### Best Practices

Explain the professional practices demonstrated in this implementation.

### Common Beginner Mistakes

Point out mistakes developers often make when implementing similar features.

### Next Learning Goal

Recommend what I should learn next before moving to the next milestone.

---

## Code Explanations

Whenever you write a complex function:

- Break it into logical sections.
- Explain each section.
- Explain important variables.
- Explain why certain decisions were made.
- Explain edge cases.
- Explain performance considerations if applicable.

---

## Refactoring

Whenever you refactor code:

Explain:

- What was wrong with the old implementation.
- Why the new implementation is better.
- Whether readability, scalability, security, or performance improved.
- Any trade-offs introduced.

---

## Professional Development

Throughout the project, continuously teach:

- Clean Code
- SOLID Principles
- DRY
- KISS
- Separation of Concerns
- API Design
- Database Design
- Security Best Practices
- Performance Optimization
- System Design
- Testing
- Git Best Practices
- Deployment Practices

Explain these concepts naturally as they become relevant.

---

## Encourage Learning

Do not solve everything without explanation.

Help me understand the reasoning behind every architectural decision.

Whenever appropriate, suggest a small exercise or improvement that I can implement myself to reinforce my understanding.

---

## Communication Style

Be concise but educational.

Prioritize teaching over speed.

Explain your reasoning instead of only providing code.

When appropriate, use simple diagrams, flowcharts, or examples to improve understanding.

Your goal is to make me capable of building and maintaining TrekPal independently while following professional software engineering practices.

Instructions for Cursor / Claude (Phase 3 roadmap — read first)

These Milestone 1–8 items are the **active** product roadmap (**Phase 3 — Ship readiness**).
Phase 1 (P1-M1…P1-M10) and Phase 2 (R1–R12) are **complete** — see `MILESTONES_PRODUCT.md`.
In docs, Phase 3 IDs may also be called **S1–S8** (same order as Milestone 1–8 here).

Work one milestone at a time, in the order given below. Do not start the next milestone until the current one is confirmed done. After finishing each milestone, stop and explain it in detail before moving on. For every milestone, give me:
What changed — plain-English summary of what was built/modified, and why it serves the milestone's stated goal or addresses its stated risk.
Files touched — every file created or edited, with a one-line note on what changed in each.
How to verify it — exact steps I can take (clicks, URLs, endpoints, commands, logs) to confirm the change works myself.
Trade-offs or shortcuts taken — anything simplified, deferred, or approximated, and why.
What's still not done — anything in scope you didn't fully get to.
Do not silently skip acceptance criteria. Each milestone has an "Acceptance" bar — if it can't be fully met, say so explicitly rather than marking it done.
Ask before making structural decisions not specified here (new cloud vendors, paid SaaS beyond what's named, schema changes beyond what's described, restructuring folders). Small implementation choices within a milestone don't need approval; architectural ones do.
Every migration must be reversible. Test alembic downgrade -1 after writing each new migration, not just the upgrade path.
Preserve the High Lodge brand (alpine mist greens, Fraunces + Manrope, no purple/glow clichés) and respect prefers-reduced-motion in every new UI addition.
No compromises on quality or correctness within a milestone's own scope — no placeholder secrets in git, no skipped HTTPS/cookie checks, no "deploy later" inside a deploy milestone — but don't let one milestone balloon into rebuilding unrelated product features (ML, maps depth, etc.).

Why this order

Production env and cookie/auth correctness come first so deploy does not bake in insecure defaults. Deploy comes next so there is a real URL to prove CI against. CI then locks the bar so regressions cannot ship silently. External AI resilience and monitoring follow once the app is reachable by strangers. Legal/trust pages are required before inviting a wide audience. The launch QA gate is last — a hard stop that says "we actually walked the product on a phone," not just "code exists."

The lens: goal-based UX (applies throughout)

Every screen should still answer: "What is the user trying to accomplish right now, and does this UI get them there in the fewest confident steps?" Phase 3 adds an ops lens: "If this fails at 2am, can we detect it and can the user still finish a safe next step?"

Milestone 1 — Production env & secrets hardening

Track: Backend/DevOps · Effort: Medium Risk addressed: local `.env` habits leaking into production (weak JWT, open CORS, ML routes on, cookies without Secure).

Document a production env checklist in `docs/PRODUCTION_ENV.md`: required vars, forbidden defaults, and what must differ from local Docker.
Harden config validation so the API refuses to start in `ENV=production` (or equivalent) if JWT secret is missing/too short, CORS is `*`, or `ENABLE_INTERNAL_ML` is true without an explicit override flag.
Ensure `.env.example` lists every required production key with safe placeholders (never real secrets). Confirm `.env` stays gitignored.

Acceptance: a reviewer can follow `docs/PRODUCTION_ENV.md` alone and know exactly which secrets to set; starting the API with a deliberately bad production config fails loudly with a clear error.

Milestone 2 — Production auth cookies (cross-origin ready)

Track: Backend/Security · Effort: Medium Risk addressed: httpOnly JWTs that work on localhost but break or weaken when frontend and API are on different HTTPS hosts.

Audit cookie flags (`HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite`, path, max-age) for a split deploy (e.g. Vercel frontend + Railway/Render API).
Document the exact cookie + CORS matrix for local vs production in the production env doc.
Add a minimal smoke path: login → authenticated `/trek/history` (or `/auth/me`) succeeds with credentials included from the frontend origin.

Acceptance: documented cookie/CORS settings for production; login session works against the intended production topology (or a staging pair that mirrors it). No auth token stored in `localStorage` for the session cookie path.

Milestone 3 — Production deploy (API + DB + frontend)

Track: DevOps · Effort: High Risk addressed: TrekPal only runs on one laptop via Docker Compose + `npm run dev`.

Deploy PostgreSQL + FastAPI API to a hosted environment; run `alembic upgrade head` as part of release.
Deploy the Next.js frontend to a hosted environment with `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` pointing at the public API.
Write `docs/DEPLOY.md` with exact steps (or IaC notes) to recreate the deploy from a clean machine.
Smoke the public URLs: health/root, signup or login, `/treks`, plan-trip generate or checklist once.

Acceptance: a stranger on another network can open the public frontend URL, sign up or log in, and see the trek catalog without SSH-ing into your PC.

Milestone 4 — CI pipeline (tests + build on every PR)

Track: Engineering/DevOps · Effort: Medium Risk addressed: regressions only discovered when someone happens to run the app locally.

Add GitHub Actions (or equivalent) that on pull request / push to `main`: run backend `pytest` (product rules + smoke if secrets allow), and frontend `lint` + `build`.
Fail the pipeline on test/lint/build failure. Cache dependencies where reasonable.
Document how to run the same checks locally in `README.md` or `docs/CI.md`.

Acceptance: an intentional failing test or broken frontend build turns the CI check red; green CI is required before merge to `main` (branch protection recommended, not optional if you control the repo settings).

Milestone 5 — External AI & weather resilience

Track: Backend/Reliability · Effort: Medium Risk addressed: Groq outage or missing key makes chat/planner feel "broken" with vague errors; Open-Meteo failures leave weather panels empty without guidance.

Ensure chat and trip-planner paths return clear, user-safe errors when Groq is down/unconfigured, and that rule-based fallback remains distinguishable (already partly done — verify end-to-end in prod-like config).
Add a simple `/health` or `/health/deps` style endpoint (or extend existing health) that reports DB up and whether Groq/Open-Meteo were reachable in a cheap check — without leaking secrets.
Frontend: chat / planner / weather empty and error states must name the dependency failure in plain language and offer a next action (retry, use checklist, open knowledge).

Acceptance: with Groq key removed or forced to fail, the user can still complete a packing checklist path; UI never shows a raw stack trace; health output is enough to see "DB ok / Groq fail."

Milestone 6 — Observability (errors + uptime)

Track: Observability · Effort: Medium Risk addressed: production failures are invisible until a user DMs you.

Add error tracking for API and frontend (e.g. Sentry, or another agreed tool — ask before adding a paid vendor if free tier is insufficient).
Wire the public health endpoint to an uptime check (GitHub-scheduled ping, Better Stack free tier, or similar) or document a manual daily check if automation is deferred with explicit sign-off.
Ensure structured logs from Phase 2 still appear in the hosted API logs and include request/route context without dumping passwords or JWTs.

Acceptance: a forced 500 on a test route (or staging toggle) creates a visible error event; you can answer "is TrekPal up?" from health/uptime without opening the laptop Docker app.

Milestone 7 — Legal & trust pages (public launch hygiene)

Track: Frontend/Content · Effort: Low-Medium Risk addressed: public users have nowhere to find privacy, terms, or "this is not medical/legal advice / verify permits" commitments.

Add `/privacy` and `/terms` (or equivalent) with plain-language TrekPal-specific copy: accounts, cookies, AI answers, location/weather data, no emergency guarantee.
Link them from footer (and signup if appropriate). Strengthen persistent "verify before travel" / not medical advice disclaimers on chat, planner, and maps without cluttering the hero.
Do not invent fake company legal entities — keep copy honest for a student/indie product.

Acceptance: a skeptical user can open Privacy and Terms from the footer on desktop and mobile; signup and AI surfaces still show non-advice / verify-before-travel language.

Milestone 8 — Launch QA gate (phone + smoke checklist)

Track: QA/Product · Effort: Medium No-compromise bar: if it was not walked on a real phone and a written checklist, it is not "shipped."

Write `docs/LAUNCH_QA.md` with a pass/fail checklist: signup onboarding, treks → plan, checklist save, itinerary generate (or documented fallback), history explainability, maps trust copy, chat rate-limit message, privacy/terms links, login cookie persistence after refresh.
Run the checklist on a real mid-range Android (or iPhone) over normal and throttled network; note breakages and fix blockers before marking done.
Record date, device, and result in the doc (or a short QA note linked from `MILESTONES_PRODUCT.md`).

Acceptance: `docs/LAUNCH_QA.md` exists with every item checked pass (or fail with linked fix PR); at least one real-device pass is recorded. Only then may Phase 3 be marked complete.

Remember: explain in detail after finishing each milestone, per the instructions at the top of this file, before starting the next one. This is the single unified roadmap — frontend, backend, and ops work are interleaved by dependency, not split into parallel tracks to "finish everything at once."
